          "content": "Board: Arduino Nano 33 BLE Sense, Arduino mbed core\n\n<handout>\n# Lab 3: Hardware PWM (mock handout)\n\n> Mock handout written for testing `grader make-plan`. It is not an official course\n> document.\n\n## Goal\n\nGenerate a **1 kHz** square wave with a **25 % duty cycle** on pin **D5** of your\nArduino Nano 33 BLE Sense.\n\n## Requirements\n\n1. The signal on D5 must have a frequency of 1 kHz and a duty cycle of 25 %.\n2. The signal must start on its own after reset. No serial input or button press is\n   needed.\n3. You must use one of the nRF52840's **hardware timers / PWM peripherals**. Solutions\n   that toggle the pin from `loop()` with `delay()` or `delayMicroseconds()`, or that use\n   `analogWrite()`, will lose points at the TA review.\n\n## Submission\n\nSubmit a single sketch folder containing your `.ino` file (and any `.cpp`/`.h` files it\nuses). Comment the register values or constants you chose and explain how you computed\nthem.\n\n## Hints\n\n* The PWM output must be stable soon after boot; the grader starts measuring shortly\n  after reset.\n* Check your prescaler and counter-top values carefully: being off by a factor of two is\n  the most common mistake.\n</handout>\n\n<requirements>\n[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"pwm_frequency\",\n    \"requirement\": \"The signal on pin D5 must have a frequency of 1 kHz.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"pwm_duty_cycle\",\n    \"requirement\": \"The signal on pin D5 must have a duty cycle of 25%.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"auto_start\",\n    \"requirement\": \"The PWM signal on D5 must start automatically after reset without any serial input or button press.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"stable_after_boot\",\n    \"requirement\": \"The PWM signal on D5 must be stable (correct frequency and duty cycle) within 50 ms after reset.\"\n  }\n]\n</requirements>\n\n<student_values>\nnone\n</student_values>\n\n<student_code untrusted=\"true\">\n=== submission_06.ino ===\n   1| /*\n   2|   Lab 3 - Hardware PWM\n   3|   1 kHz square wave, 25% duty, on D5.\n   4| \n   5|   D5 = P1.13\n   6| \n   7|   PWM peripheral clock: 16 MHz\n   8|   PRESCALER = DIV_16 -> 1 MHz, 1 us resolution\n   9|   COUNTERTOP = 1000  -> 1000 us period = 1 kHz\n  10|   compare    = 250   -> 250 us high = 25 %\n  11| \n  12|   The duty value is constant so I made the table const.\n  13| */\n  14| \n  15| #include <Arduino.h>\n  16| \n  17| static const uint16_t PWM_TABLE[] = { 250 | 0x8000 };\n  18| \n  19| void setup() {\n  20|   NRF_PWM3->PSEL.OUT[0] = (1UL << PWM_PSEL_OUT_PORT_Pos) | (13UL << PWM_PSEL_OUT_PIN_Pos);\n  21| \n  22|   NRF_PWM3->ENABLE     = PWM_ENABLE_ENABLE_Enabled;\n  23|   NRF_PWM3->MODE       = PWM_MODE_UPDOWN_Up;\n  24|   NRF_PWM3->PRESCALER  = PWM_PRESCALER_PRESCALER_DIV_16;\n  25|   NRF_PWM3->COUNTERTOP = 1000;\n  26|   NRF_PWM3->LOOP       = 0;\n  27|   NRF_PWM3->DECODER    = (PWM_DECODER_LOAD_Common << PWM_DECODER_LOAD_Pos) |\n  28|                          (PWM_DECODER_MODE_RefreshCount << PWM_DECODER_MODE_Pos);\n  29| \n  30|   NRF_PWM3->SEQ[0].PTR      = (uint32_t)PWM_TABLE;\n  31|   NRF_PWM3->SEQ[0].CNT      = sizeof(PWM_TABLE) / sizeof(PWM_TABLE[0]);\n  32|   NRF_PWM3->SEQ[0].REFRESH  = 0;\n  33|   NRF_PWM3->SEQ[0].ENDDELAY = 0;\n  34| \n  35|   NRF_PWM3->TASKS_SEQSTART[0] = 1;\n  36| }\n  37| \n  38| void loop() {\n  39| }\n</student_code>"
